# Template. Copy to .env (or .env.local) and fill in — never commit the filled
# file. .gitignore covers .env*, with an explicit exception for this template so
# the list of required variables stays in version control.
#
# With none of these set the site still runs. Inquiries are written to the
# server console instead of being delivered, and the visitor is shown success
# — that is the local-development path. In production, set at least one of the
# two delivery channels below, or real inquiries will be lost.
#
# These are local only. Vercel does not read this file: set the same keys under
# Settings → Environment Variables, and redeploy.

# ── Public site URL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonical host for canonical tags, sitemap.xml, robots.txt, llms.txt and all
# OpenGraph URLs. Must be the host that actually serves content: the apex
# redirects to www, so naming the apex here would point every canonical at a
# redirect. Read at BUILD time — changing it requires a redeploy, not a restart.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.valnexindustries.com

# ── Delivery channel 1: database ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Apply supabase/migrations/*.sql first (supabase db push). Both migrations:
# 0002 drops a stale CHECK constraint that would reject every division except
# "unsure".
#
# Replace the project ref as well as the key. A real key against a placeholder
# URL counts as "configured", so the insert is attempted, fails, and the
# visitor sees an error.
#
# The service role key bypasses RLS, so it is server-only — never prefix it
# with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ── Delivery channel 2: email (Nodemailer over SMTP) ───────────────────────
# Sent through the company's own Microsoft 365 mailbox, so Microsoft signs the
# message and SPF/DKIM pass with NO DNS changes. The root SPF record is
# `v=spf1 include:secureserver.net -all` -- a hard fail -- so a third-party
# sender would have needed its own records; this route avoids that entirely.
#
# INQUIRY_FROM_EMAIL should equal SMTP_USER: Exchange Online rejects a From
# address the authenticated mailbox has no SendAs right over.
#
# SMTP_PASS: Microsoft has been retiring Basic auth for SMTP client submission.
# If plain user/password is refused, the options are an app password (needs
# security defaults off), or OAuth2 via an Azure app registration. Verify this
# works before launch rather than discovering it on the first real inquiry.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.office365.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=contact@valnexindustries.com
SMTP_PASS=
INQUIRY_FROM_EMAIL=contact@valnexindustries.com
INQUIRY_TO_EMAIL=contact@valnexindustries.com
